## 2. System Architecture

DeviceDNA follows a **4-layer, stream-processing architecture**. Data flows continuously from bottom to top:

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Layer 1 — Data Infrastructure"
        PG[(PostgreSQL 16)]
        IF[(InfluxDB 2.7)]
        RD[(Redis 7)]
        KF[Apache Kafka]
        ZK[Zookeeper]
    end

    subgraph "Layer 2 — Telemetry Simulator"
        SIM[Python Simulator]
        DP[Device Profiles × 6 classes]
        TG[Traffic Generator]
        ATK[Attack Scenario Injector]
    end

    subgraph "Layer 3 — ML Backend API"
        FA[FastAPI + Uvicorn]
        TS[Telemetry Service]
        FE[Feature Extraction Engine]
        TE[Trust Score Engine]
        VAE[VAE Digital Twin]
        ISO[Isolation Forest]
        LSTM_M[LSTM Temporal]
        GNN_M[GNN GraphSAGE]
        CUSUM[CUSUM Drift Engine]
        DNA[DNA Fingerprint Service]
    end

    subgraph "Layer 4 — SOC Dashboard"
        NX[Next.js 14 + React 18]
        D3[D3.js Network Map]
        RC[Recharts Timeline]
        HM[Drift Heatmap]
        WS[Socket.IO Client]
    end

    SIM -->|"Produces JSON flows"| KF
    KF -->|"raw-flows topic"| TS
    TS -->|"Broadcasts alerts"| WS
    TS -.->|"NOT YET WIRED"| FE
    FE -.->|"14D vectors"| TE
    TE -->|"Pillar 1: 35%"| VAE
    TE -->|"Pillar 2: 25%"| ISO
    TE -->|"Pillar 3: 20%"| CUSUM
    TE -.->|"Stubbed: 0%"| LSTM_M
    TE -.->|"Stubbed: 0%"| GNN_M
    TE -.->|"Scores"| RD
    TE -.->|"History"| IF
    FA -->|"REST API"| NX
    WS -->|"Live events"| NX
    PG -->|"Device/Alert records"| FA
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Dashed lines (-.->)  represent connections that are **architecturally planned but not yet wired** in the current codebase. Solid lines represent working connections.

---

### 2.1 Layer 1 — Data Infrastructure (Docker Compose)

All infrastructure runs inside Docker containers orchestrated by [docker-compose.yml](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/docker-compose.yml). A single `docker compose up` boots the entire data layer.

| Service | Image | Port | Role |
|---------|-------|------|------|
| **PostgreSQL 16** | `postgres:16-alpine` | 5432 | Relational store for device records, alerts, platform settings |
| **InfluxDB 2.7** | `influxdb:2.7` | 8086 | Time-series DB for telemetry history and feature vectors |
| **Redis 7** | `redis:7-alpine` | 6379 | In-memory cache for real-time trust score lookups |
| **Apache Kafka** | `confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.5.0` | 9092 / 29092 | High-throughput message queue for streaming telemetry |
| **Zookeeper** | `confluentinc/cp-zookeeper:7.5.0` | 2181 | Kafka cluster coordination |

**Database Schema** ([init.sql](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/backend/db_init/init.sql)):
- `devices` — UUID PK, name, MAC, IP, device_class (enum of 6 types), VLAN, status (online/isolated/sandboxed), baseline tracking timestamps
- `alerts` — UUID PK, FK to device, severity (critical/high/medium/low), headline, SHAP values (JSONB), response action tracking
- `platform_settings` — Key-value config store, seeded with `response_mode: "advisory"` and trust thresholds

---

### 2.2 Layer 2 — Telemetry Simulator

Since we can't safely test on production hospital/factory networks, a Python-based simulator generates realistic network traffic for 50 IoT devices across 6 classes.

**Source files:**
- [device_profiles.py](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/backend/simulator/device_profiles.py) — Defines 6 device class behavioral profiles
- [traffic_generator.py](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/backend/simulator/traffic_generator.py) — Generates individual flow records
- [attack_scenarios.py](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/backend/simulator/attack_scenarios.py) — Injects 4 distinct attack patterns
- [main.py](file:///d:/coding_files/Projects/DeviceDNA/backend/simulator/main.py) — Async Kafka producer loop

**The 6 Device Classes and Their Behavioral DNA:**

| Class | Count | VLAN | Dominant Protocol | Avg Flows/5min | Avg Bytes/Flow | External Ratio |
|-------|-------|------|-------------------|----------------|----------------|----------------|
| Camera | 12 | 20 | RTSP (45%) | 80 ± 15 | 15,000 ± 5,000 | 15% ± 5% |
| Sensor | 10 | 10 | MQTT (60%) | 20 ± 5 | 500 ± 200 | 1% ± 1% |
| Thermostat | 8 | 10 | HTTPS (50%) | 15 ± 5 | 1,000 ± 400 | 30% ± 10% |
| Access Control | 6 | 20 | HTTPS (40%) | 30 ± 10 | 2,000 ± 800 | 10% ± 5% |
| Medical | 8 | 30 | HL7 (35%) | 40 ± 12 | 8,000 ± 3,000 | 5% ± 3% |
| Industrial | 6 | 40 | Modbus (40%) | 50 ± 15 | 3,000 ± 1,000 | 2% ± 1% |

Each device is assigned a unique ID (`SIM-0001` through `SIM-0050`), a random MAC address, an IP within its VLAN subnet, and a set of known internal/external peer IPs.

**Traffic generation** uses Gaussian distributions (`random.gauss(mean, std)`) seeded from each class profile to create statistically realistic flow records with proper protocol distributions, byte counts, and external traffic ratios.

**The 4 Attack Scenarios** (injected every 100 cycles ≈ 50 seconds):

| # | Name | Target | Behavior | Detected By |
|---|------|--------|----------|-------------|
| 1 | **Botnet C2 Beaconing** | Camera (SIM-0001) | Contacts 3 new malicious external IPs on port 4444 with 128-byte beacon payloads | VAE + Isolation Forest |
| 2 | **Slow Data Exfiltration** | Sensor (SIM-0013) | Upload volume jumps to 5,000–8,000 bytes (vs 500 normal) over HTTPS | CUSUM Drift Engine |
| 3 | **Lateral Movement** | Medical devices | Two medical devices that never communicated start SSH connections (port 22) | GNN GraphSAGE |
| 4 | **NLP Policy Violation** | Thermostat | Contacts known TOR exit node IP (185.220.101.43) | Policy Engine |

